# NBC and Renewed pages (deepest pages).

def build_nbc(page, H, book_page):
    return [book_page(page, H, "nbc",
      "The Night Before Christmas with the Nativity Story | Christian Christmas Picture Book (Hardcover)",
      "A Christian Christmas picture book for children: Moore's Night Before Christmas poem plus the nativity story told from the Bible. 78-page hardcover gift by Poss Jonah. See real inside pages.",
      "The Night Before Christmas, with the Nativity Story: a Christian Christmas Picture Book",
      "Two Christmas stories in one hardcover. First comes Clement Clarke Moore's poem, word for word, with Santa Zeb, eight little reindeer and the Possum Holler friends. Then Poss Jonah sits the Holler kids down by the fire and tells them why we have Christmas at all: the story of Jesus' birth, retold for children from the Bible.",
      '<p>It is made to be the book a family pulls out every December: a fun read-aloud for Christmas Eve that keeps Christ at the center of the season.</p>',
      "Look inside: real pages from the poem and the nativity",
      [("nbc/p08", "Jeri Lynn the rabbit reads The Night Before Christmas to the Possum Holler kids by the fireplace", "<b>Page 8, the poem begins.</b> Jeri Lynn opens the book for the Holler kids by the fire."),
       ("nbc/p10", "Opening stanza of the Night Before Christmas poem framed in holly and pinecones", "<b>Page 10.</b> Moore's opening stanza, “’Twas the night before Christmas…”, set in large type."),
       ("nbc/p16", "A young rabbit at a frosty window watches Santa's sleigh and eight reindeer cross the moon", "<b>Page 16.</b> Eight tiny reindeer across a snowy sky."),
       ("nbc/p19", "Santa Zeb, a bear with a full white beard, drives his sleigh through falling snow", "<b>Page 19.</b> Santa Zeb, white beard and all, in his sleigh."),
       ("nbc/p23", "Santa's sleigh and reindeer fly past a full moon over a snowy forest", "<b>Page 23.</b> “Up to the house-top the coursers they flew.”"),
       ("nbc/p38", "Poss Jonah the possum tells the Holler kids the story of the first Christmas around a campfire", "<b>Page 38, the nativity begins.</b> Poss asks the kids, “Y’all know why we have Christmas?”"),
       ("nbc/p50", "The angel Gabriel visits Mary in a stone house, from Luke chapter 1, in a children's nativity book", "<b>Page 50.</b> Gabriel visits Mary (Luke 1)."),
       ("nbc/p55", "An angel speaks to Joseph in a dream, from Matthew chapter 1, children's Bible story illustration", "<b>Page 55.</b> An angel tells Joseph to name the child Jesus (Matthew 1)."),
       ("nbc/p60", "Mary holds baby Jesus in the stable beside Joseph, with Luke 2:7 quoted in a speech bubble", "<b>Page 60.</b> Jesus is born and laid in a manger (Luke 2:7). “Why it matters: Jesus came for everybody, rich or poor.”"),
       ("nbc/p62", "An angel brings good news of great joy to the shepherds at night outside Bethlehem", "<b>Page 62.</b> Good news for the shepherds (Luke 2)."),
       ("nbc/p68", "The wise men bring gifts to young Jesus with Mary and Joseph in a children's nativity picture book", "<b>Page 68.</b> The wise men bring their gifts (Matthew 2).")],
      """
<h2>What's inside this Christian Christmas book for children</h2>
<p>The book has three parts, and each one does a different job on Christmas Eve.</p>
<h3>1. A Holler family Christmas Eve (pages 1–9)</h3>
<p>The story opens in Possum Holler on Christmas Eve. Jeri Lynn gathers the young critters around the fireplace and opens a book. It's a gentle way in for little listeners, who see kids like them settling down to hear a story before bed.</p>
<h3>2. Moore's poem, as he wrote it (pages 10–37)</h3>
<p>“A Visit from St. Nicholas,” better known as “The Night Before Christmas,” was first published in 1823 and is credited to Clement Clarke Moore. All fourteen stanzas appear in Moore's own words, each one set in large type across from a full-page painting. The Holler folks act out the poem: the stockings, the clatter on the lawn, the eight tiny reindeer, and Santa Zeb with his round belly and snow-white beard.</p>
<h3>3. The nativity story from the Bible (pages 38–71)</h3>
<p>Then Poss Jonah takes over. Sitting by the fire with the Holler kids, he tells them it's “the best story ever told.” Each of the next 34 pages pairs a realistic painting of the Bible scene with a speech bubble from one of the Holler critters. The bubbles retell the story in plain words for kids ten and under. Short Scripture quotes come from the World English Bible, and each page gives its reference, like Luke 2:7. Many pages end with a one-line <em>Why it matters</em> for kids to remember, such as “Jesus came for everybody, rich or poor.”</p>
<p>The nativity part follows the Gospels in order, beginning with the promises of a coming King in Isaiah and Micah. Next come Zechariah and Elizabeth, Gabriel's visit to Mary, Joseph's dream, the trip to Bethlehem, the manger, the shepherds and the angels, Simeon and Anna in the Temple, the wise men, and the family's escape to Egypt. On the last page, the whole Holler gathers around the true meaning of Christmas.</p>

<h2>Why families choose a nativity picture book that includes the classic poem</h2>
<p>Many Christian parents love the cozy tradition of “The Night Before Christmas” but don't want Santa to be the whole story. This book doesn't make you choose. Kids get the poem they'll hear everywhere in December, and on the very next page they learn why the day exists. The two parts are kept clearly separate. The poem is presented as a fun poem, and the nativity is presented as the true story from the Bible.</p>

<h2>How to use it for Advent, homeschool, and Sunday school</h2>
<h3>An easy Advent read-aloud plan</h3>
<p>The nativity section breaks naturally into short evening readings. One way to spread it across the weeks before Christmas:</p>
<table><tr><th>Evening</th><th>Read</th><th>Bible passage to open with</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Poss starts the story; God's promises of a King</td><td>Isaiah 9:2, 6–7; Micah 5:2</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Zechariah and Elizabeth</td><td>Luke 1:5–25</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Gabriel visits Mary</td><td>Luke 1:26–38</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Mary visits Elizabeth; John is born</td><td>Luke 1:39–80</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>An angel visits Joseph</td><td>Matthew 1:18–25</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>The road to Bethlehem and the manger</td><td>Luke 2:1–7</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Shepherds and the angels</td><td>Luke 2:8–20</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Simeon and Anna</td><td>Luke 2:22–38</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>The wise men; the escape to Egypt</td><td>Matthew 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Christmas Eve</td><td>The whole poem, then the last nativity page</td><td>Luke 2:10–11</td></tr></table>
<h3>For homeschool families</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Poetry memorization:</b> Moore's poem has strong rhythm and rhyme. Let children learn the opening stanza and recite it on Christmas Eve.</li>
<li><b>Bible memory:</b> Pick one <em>Why it matters</em> line or one quoted verse a week to memorize.</li>
<li><b>Narration:</b> After a reading, ask your child to tell the scene back in their own words, then draw it.</li>
<li><b>Compare and contrast:</b> Older kids can talk through how a fun poem and a true account are different kinds of writing.</li>
</ul>
<h3>For Sunday school and children's church</h3>
<p>The big paintings read well to a small group. The Scripture reference on each page makes it easy to open a Bible next to the book. The nativity pages work on their own as a short lesson on any Sunday in Advent.</p>

<div class="note"><b>Ordering for Christmas:</b> On Nov 18, 2026, Amazon listed this hardcover as “usually ships within 8 days.” If it is meant to go under the tree, order early and check the delivery date Amazon shows at checkout.</div>
""",
      [("Is this a Christian book or a Santa book?", "Both, kept clearly apart. The first part is Moore's classic poem with Santa Zeb. The second, longer part is the story of Jesus' birth retold for children from the Bible, with Scripture quotes from the World English Bible."),
       ("What ages is it for?", "Amazon lists a reading age of 3–8 years. Little ones enjoy the pictures and rhythm of the poem. Early readers can read the nativity speech bubbles themselves, and the bubbles are written for kids ten and under."),
       ("Is the poem changed?", "No. The book notes that Moore's words appear as he wrote them, told by the folks of Possum Holler."),
       ("Which Bible translation is used?", "Quotations in the nativity section are from the World English Bible, a public-domain modern English translation."),
       ("How big is the book?", "It is an 8.25 × 11 inch hardcover with 78 full-color pages. Amazon lists the shipped dimensions as about 8.5 × 11.2 inches."),
       ("Can I buy it here?", "No. The buy buttons on this page go straight to the book's Amazon page. Amazon prints, sells, and ships it, and handles returns.")],
      ["renewed", "holler", "hcolor"])]


def build_renewed(page, H, book_page):
    return [book_page(page, H, "renewed",
      "Renewed Every Morning: Daily Devotional for Women | 365-Day Bible Verse Book (Hardcover)",
      "A 365-day Bible verse devotional for women: one verse, a short reflection and a one-line prayer for every date. Hardcover, 396 pages, by Poss Jonah. See real inside pages.",
      "Renewed Every Morning: a 365-Day Bible Verse Devotional for Women",
      "A quiet, two-minute start to the day. Each dated page has one short Bible verse, a brief reflection written for real life, and either a one-line prayer or a short phrase to carry with you. Open to today's date in any year and begin.",
      '<p>The title comes from Lamentations 3:22–23: “his mercies don’t fail. They are new every morning. Great is your faithfulness.” That is the verse on January 1.</p>',
      "Look inside: real pages from the devotional",
      [("renewed/p008", "January 1 devotional page with Lamentations 3:22-23, new every morning, a reflection and a prayer", "<b>January 1, “New mercies.”</b> Lamentations 3:22–23, the verse that names the book."),
       ("renewed/p050", "February 11 devotional page with Romans 5:8 and a short prayer", "<b>February 11, “Loved and known.”</b> Romans 5:8 with a short reflection and prayer."),
       ("renewed/p343", "November 18 devotional page with John 15:13 on friendship and love", "<b>November 18.</b> John 15:13, from the November theme “People who walk with us.”"),
       ("renewed/p382", "December 25 Christmas Day devotional page in a daily Bible verse book for women", "<b>December 25.</b> The Christmas Day page."),
       ("renewed/p005", "How to use this book page explaining the dated daily devotional format", "<b>How to use this book.</b> A dated companion, not a plan that has to start on January 1."),
       ("renewed/p006", "Monthly themes page listing a devotional theme for each month of the year", "<b>Monthly themes.</b> A theme for each month, from New mercies to Light, waiting, and joy.")],
      """
<h2>What each day's page holds</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>One verse,</b> printed in full from the World English Bible, a public-domain modern English translation. No verse repeats anywhere in the book.</li>
<li><b>A short reflection,</b> usually three or four sentences. It's honest about tired mornings and full calendars, and it points back to the verse.</li>
<li><b>A one-line prayer</b> (“A PRAYER”) or a short phrase to hold onto (“CARRY THIS TODAY”).</li>
</ul>
<p>A day takes about two minutes. There's a bonus page for February 29 after February 28, so the book works in leap years too.</p>

<h2>A theme for every month</h2>
<table><tr><th>Month</th><th>Theme</th><th>Month</th><th>Theme</th></tr>
<tr><td>January</td><td>New mercies</td><td>July</td><td>A steady courage</td></tr>
<tr><td>February</td><td>Loved and known</td><td>August</td><td>Enough for today</td></tr>
<tr><td>March</td><td>Strength for ordinary days</td><td>September</td><td>Wisdom for the next step</td></tr>
<tr><td>April</td><td>Renewal and second chances</td><td>October</td><td>A grateful heart</td></tr>
<tr><td>May</td><td>A gentle heart</td><td>November</td><td>People who walk with us</td></tr>
<tr><td>June</td><td>Peace in the middle of it</td><td>December</td><td>Light, waiting, and joy</td></tr></table>

<h2>Who this women's devotional is for</h2>
<p><b>The busy mom,</b> including the homeschool mom who teaches all day and wants a moment in the Word before the kids wake up. <b>The woman coming back to faith,</b> who wants something gentle and short. <b>The faithful Bible reader</b> who wants one verse to think about between longer readings. And <b>anyone who gives gifts to women,</b> because a dated book can be started on any day, so it works as a gift in December, on a birthday, or on Mother's Day.</p>

<h2>Ways to use it</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Morning quiet time:</b> keep it by the coffee pot and read the day's page first.</li>
<li><b>Women's Bible study or small group:</b> everyone reads the same date, then shares what stood out.</li>
<li><b>Journaling:</b> copy the verse by hand and write one sentence back to God.</li>
<li><b>With older kids:</b> read the verse aloud at breakfast; the reflections are written for grown-ups, but the verses fit the whole family.</li>
<li><b>Online companion:</b> as the book notes, today's verse also appears at <a href="https://verse.help-pal-apps.com/">verse.help-pal-apps.com</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A thoughtful Christian gift for women</h2>
<p>It's a compact 5.5 × 8.5 inch hardcover with 396 pages. It works as a Christmas present for a mother, grandmother, sister, teacher, pastor's wife, or friend, or as a New Year gift for someone ready for a fresh start. The January 1 page is “New mercies,” but she can open the book to whatever date it is and begin there.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Ordering for Christmas:</b> On Nov 18, 2026, Amazon listed this hardcover as “usually ships within 9 days.” Check the delivery date Amazon shows at checkout.</div>
""",
      [("Do I have to start on January 1?", "No. Each page is dated, and the book is meant to be opened to today's date in any year. Start the day it arrives."),
       ("Which Bible translation does it use?", "The World English Bible, a public-domain modern English translation. Each verse shows its reference."),
       ("Is it a reading plan through the whole Bible?", "No. It's one short verse a day with a reflection, about two minutes. It works well next to a longer reading plan."),
       ("Is there a page for February 29?", "Yes, a bonus page follows February 28. In other years, just turn past it."),
       ("How big is it?", "It is a 5.5 × 8.5 inch hardcover with 396 pages. Amazon lists the shipped size as about 5.7 × 8.7 inches and the weight as about 1.3 pounds."),
       ("Is it for teens?", "It's written for adult women, but the verses and prayers suit older teen girls too. Parents can look inside using the pages above.")],
      ["nbc", "holler", "states"])]
